---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ───────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against office-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md
// (workbook row 26, primary `office cleaning services` 1,500/mo, cluster
// 3,010/mo — the second largest commercial cluster in the workbook). Section
// numbers in the comments below are that brief's.
//
// Implemented: the approved title and meta, a new H1 carrying the mapped
// phrase with the old one demoted into the hero lead, the mapped phrase in
// the opening paragraph, the approved CTA in all four places, the narrowed
// comparison table with an office subject label, the renamed FAQ headings,
// the full-footprint town list and the em dash recast in non-branded FAQ 3.
//
// NO BLOCKING CONFLICT on this page. The approved CTA and the page agree; the
// only open wording question is the walk-through spelling, recorded at
// `walkthroughCta` below.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (the workbook
// wants /commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning, the build is flat — a redirect
// decision for the build team), the canonical domain, FAQ schema, the
// header's competing "Get an instant quote", and hiding the testimonial block
// until real quotes exist. The stray double space in the old H1 is gone
// because the line that carried it moved into the lead.
//
// Office cleaning — the first and most-searched of the THIRTEEN "by type of
// premises" leaf pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts,
// "By type of premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied, and needs sign-off before launch. Where a claim depends on a
// fact only TLB holds, it ships as a visible [bracket] per house convention
// rather than as a plausible guess.
//
// This is the cluster's DEFAULT page and it carries the fullest structure of
// the thirteen: the hub's own meta description names office cleaning, and the
// hub's §3 card 1 CTA already points here. The twelve siblings each drop or
// swap sections below the trust strip so the cluster does not read as one
// page with the nouns changed — but every one of them keeps the hero,
// Backdrop(TrustBar + definition), the premises cross-link band, the service
// area band, two FAQ blocks and the closing CTA, because those six are the
// site's own convention (BFD 3.5) and not this page's invention.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, shared across all thirteen:
//   - [insured] and [police-checked] in the pathway cards. Open on the hub
//     and on both commercial service pages already. One answer fixes fifteen
//     pages.
//   - Pricing basis. Every one of the thirteen is deliberately silent on a
//     dollar figure and says "priced per visit against a written scope"
//     instead. If TLB has a real minimum charge or a per-square-metre rate,
//     it belongs in the cost FAQ on each page.
//   - Whether commercial work stops at the NSW border. `nswLocationGroups`
//     here follows the hub; footerContact still claims the Southern Gold
//     Coast. See the comment in src/data/premises.ts.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - "The same cleaner every week" is the page's central promise and it is
//     an operational commitment, not a marketing line. Confirm TLB will hold
//     it for office contracts before this ships. If the honest answer is "the
//     same small team, not always the same person", the hero lead, pathway
//     card 3 and branded FAQ 2 all need rewording together.
//   - Whether TLB supplies consumables (hand towel, toilet paper, bin
//     liners). Bracketed in the scope block and in a non-branded FAQ. This is
//     the single most common thing an office manager asks that this page
//     currently cannot answer.
//
// NOT BUILT, deliberately: before/after slider. BeforeAfter.astro needs real
// paired photography from a locked-off tripod and none exists for commercial
// work; the brief for it is written out at the reserved slot in the render.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// Pathway card 2 REUSES the hub's walkthrough frame from `../`, per
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5 ("reuse before generating; same subject = same file").
// Card 2's subject is the conversation where a scope gets agreed, which is
// exactly what the hub uses that frame to mean, and its label is the hub's
// verbatim so one photo never carries two different alt texts.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub and
// both commercial service pages.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, serviceComparisonRows } from '../../data/comparison';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Focus keyword leads the title, matching the hub's pattern and
// both commercial service pages.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Office Cleaning Services - Small & Corporate | TLB Cleaning":
// Brief §2. The approved title, verbatim. 58 of 60 characters, and it lands
// `office cleaning services` 1,500/mo contiguously while the modifier picks
// up `small office cleaning services` 150/mo and `corporate office cleaning
// services` 200/mo. The old one used 30 characters and targeted none of them.
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// Brief §3. The approved meta, verbatim, at 142 characters. The old one was
// 160 and would have been cut off.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST for the workbook owner, brief §3: this carries "office
// cleaning" but not the full mapped phrase, so the meta component cannot
// score full marks even once implemented, and it drops the region the live
// one named, which costs a little on `office cleaning services near me`
// 200/mo. Pasted as approved rather than edited, per Rule 3.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in four places per BFD §9.5:
// the hero button, pathway card 1, the button after the six scope blocks and
// the closing block. The page already standardised on a single label, so this
// is a substitution rather than a cleanup.
//
// ⚠️ LOW-PRIORITY CHANGE REQUEST for the workbook owner, brief §7. The page
// spells it "walkthrough" as one word everywhere in its own copy, including
// the booking section and the closing heading "Start with a walkthrough." The
// approved CTA hyphenates it. Both are acceptable English, but the button and
// the body copy should not disagree on the same page. Pasted as approved per
// Rule 3, and raised for a one-word decision.

// §1 Hero. The kicker carries the focus keyword so someone arriving from a
// search result gets confirmation above the H1, which is then free to be the
// benefit rather than the keyword. Same call the hub and both service pages
// make.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO, not a cut-out figure, following the call the hub made at
// TLB's direction: a studio cut-out on a plain background says "a cleaner"
// without saying commercial. Here the boardroom table, the desk bank behind
// the glass and the empty monitors are what carry it.
//
// `ratio` is 16/9 because the SOURCE is 16:9, so a box matching the file
// crops nothing. Regenerate at the same ratio if this is ever reshot, or
// change both together.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. An AI image of a person who does not exist,
// in the IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 canonical uniform (aqua polo, dark teal apron,
// teal gloves). Honest as generic service imagery and NOT presented as a
// named team member, which §7 forbids. A real photo of a real TLB cleaner on
// a real site would still do more, so treat this as a good placeholder
// rather than the finished job. True of all thirteen premises pages.
//
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `office cleaning services` 1,500/mo is worth 15
// points scored present or absent, and the trading extension answers the
// commercial buyer's first worry under BFD §8.3, which is being seen cleaning
// or having trade interrupted. The old H1 was one word short of the mapped
// phrase in every heading on the page.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §5 and §6, in that order. The demoted H1 opens the lead, so it is
// still the first thing under the headline, and the hero paragraph follows.
// Hero has no separate subheading slot — `headingLines` all render inside the
// one <h1>, which is where a second line would be wrong — so the two run as
// one lead paragraph rather than as a new component prop. The stray double
// space the old markup carried is gone with the move.
//
// One added word lands the mapped phrase contiguously inside the opening
// sentences, which is the full 10-point opening-paragraph component.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the first
// place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the synonym work
// ("commercial office cleaning", "office cleaners near me") so this one page
// answers all three queries instead of three thin pages competing.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three situations an office manager actually arrives
// with, in their own words. Same framing as the hub, re-pointed at offices.
//
// Destinations are the quote flow, the hub and /why-tlb/. A leaf that links
// only to a quote form strands the reader who is not ready to ask for one.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on "I need to know who is in the building" (description):
// ⚠️ Two brackets, both inherited from the hub. Same answer fixes
// every page in the cluster.

// §4 Scope of work. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE.
//
// commercial-cleaning.astro flags in its own source that the site tells
// readers four times to demand a written scope and never shows one. The
// commercial carpet page closed that gap for a single service; this closes it
// for the default ongoing contract, which is the harder and more valuable
// half.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard commercial practice, not supplied
// by TLB. Confirm each one and cut anything TLB does not actually do. An
// overstated scope is worse than a short one, because this is the block a
// client will hold us to.
//
// The deliberate structure is frequency, not room order: what happens every
// visit, what happens less often, and what is not included at all. An office
// manager comparing two quotes is comparing frequencies, and a flat room list
// hides exactly the difference they are trying to see.
//
// In `scopeItems`, on "Not included, and said out loud" (description):
// ⚠️ Real brackets. Consumables is the most-asked question this page
// cannot currently answer, and carpet extraction is a separate service
// with its own page.

// §5 Callout. The one thing an office manager most often gets wrong when
// comparing quotes, said as an interruption rather than as another paragraph.
//
// This is a `note`, not a `warning`: it corrects a belief rather than warning
// the reader off an action. Callout.astro's own docs draw exactly that line.

// §6 "Worth knowing". The hub's equivalent block is the strongest copy on the
// site and it works by making the reader distrust the cheapest quote rather
// than by making claims about TLB. Same job here, and on offices the lever is
// the Award: a rate below it is not a better deal, it is a liability.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and it is the same pass the hub needs.
// Cleaning Services Award 2020 (MA000022) is correct and is what the hub
// already cites. No rate is quoted here ON PURPOSE — Award rates change
// every 1 July and an undated specific goes stale silently. Recommended
// before launch: a visible "last reviewed" date on this block.

// §7 Related pages. The other twelve premises types, the two commercial
// services and the hub, from src/data/premises.ts — one list, shared by all
// thirteen, with this page filtered out of its own band.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning/');

// §9 Comparison table. The shared rows from src/data/comparison.ts, now on a
// fifth page.
//
// ⚠️ Row one is "Nine full-time cleaners employed by TLB, not subcontracted".
// That claim is load-bearing on this page in a way it is not elsewhere,
// because §6 above builds the whole argument on it. If office work is ever
// subcontracted, §6 and this table both come off together.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on `rows`:
// Brief §19, two changes inside the table. The subject label becomes the
// office rather than the home, and the holiday-lets row comes off: an office
// manager reading a row about holiday turnovers, under a header asking who
// cleans their home, is being shown a table written for a different reader.
// The other seven rows are neutral, factual and correct as they stand.

// §10 Branded FAQs. Written in the CUSTOMER'S voice, where "we" means the
// reader's business rather than TLB. That register is the hub's, and it is
// held across all thirteen premises pages deliberately: a leaf that switches
// voice reads as bolted on.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Will it be the same cleaner every week?" (answer):
// ⚠️ This is the page's central promise and an operational commitment.
// See the file header. If the honest answer is "the same small team",
// this answer, the hero lead and pathway card 3 change together.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you bring your own equipment and products?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Consumables bracket, second of two on the page.

// §11 Non-branded FAQs. The long-tail block, aimed at what people search
// rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. These claims are checkable:
//   - Cleaning Services Award 2020 (MA000022) covers commercial cleaning and
//     sets penalty rates for weekends and late shifts. Correct, and
//     consistent with the hub, which says the same.
//   - Public liability insurance is not legally mandatory for a cleaning
//     business in NSW but is universally required by commercial clients and
//     landlords. Stated carefully below for that reason.
//   - No dollar figure appears anywhere in this block, because the honest
//     answer depends on a number TLB has not supplied.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does office cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE. The most searched question on the topic, and
// the page cannot answer it without a number from TLB. A "from $X" that
// turns out to be wrong is worse than no number, and the ACCC treats
// from-pricing as a claim.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "What is included in a standard office clean?" (answer):
// Brief §25. BFD §9.2 bans em dashes outright and this was the only pair
// on the page. Recast with a colon, every word and every item kept, and
// it reads slightly better with the list at the end of the sentence
// rather than interrupting it.

// §12 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Office cleaning"). This file owns the layout.
// The notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button (§7 above). It links to the quote
    // page in Site settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // A card with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    pathways: section.pathwayCards.extend({
      cards: z.array(section.pathwayCards.shape.cards.element.extend({ cta: section.optional(section.link) })).min(1),
    }),
    scope: section.iconGrid,
    callout: section.callout,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings.
    related: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    // The columns and rows are the shared table in Site settings. The page
    // owns its framing and the label of the "who cleans" row.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable
      .pick({ heading: true, lead: true, cornerLabel: true })
      .extend({ whoCleansLabel: section.text }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
  cta: card.cta ?? walkthroughCta,
}));

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid across
        both sections. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured over this
        frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave 4.22:1 — an
        AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc carries the
        method. Same treatment as the hub, and the same on all thirteen
        premises pages. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.scope.heading}
      lead={page.scope.lead}
      note={page.scope.note}
      items={page.scope.items}
      cta={walkthroughCta}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.callout.heading} body={page.callout.body} cta={page.callout.cta} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: before and after slider (BeforeAfter.astro).

        NOT BUILT because BeforeAfter has no reserved-slot mode by design: its
        `src` is required, since a comparison slider with nothing to compare
        has no meaningful empty state. It needs real paired photography and
        none exists for commercial work.

        THE PHOTO BRIEF for this page: one office bathroom or one kitchenette,
        shot from a fixed tripod position, before and after, with NOTHING
        moved between the two frames. A shifted camera or a mug picked up
        reads as a glitch rather than as cleaning. Two pairs is plenty. Drop
        it in here, between "Worth knowing" and the related pages. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={serviceComparisonRows(page.comparison.whoCleansLabel)}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* Brief §21, the full footprint rather than the six-town NSW list. Three
        reasons: the hero promises the Tweed and this band dropped it, against
        BFD §2.1; `office cleaning services gold coast` is worth 150/mo and was
        captured at zero because the page never said Gold Coast; and the
        navigation on this very page already carries this exact list, so it is
        a template fix rather than a writing task. Same `footprintLocationGroups`
        the four earlier copy-rewrite pages use — see the block comment in
        src/data/locations.ts for why it diverges from the client's own
        fourteen-town call. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §23 and §24. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error: this block is about
        working with TLB, the next is about the service. The second heading
        also lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is the 10-point H2
        component the page previously half scored, and drops Title Case,
        which BFD §9.2 bans. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
